cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository, cite it as below. Every number in it is a simulation under stated assumptions, not a measurement."
title: "RadSlice: a simulation study of spare capacity and fault protection for small fault-tolerant hardware"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Sivaramakrishnan
    given-names: Ujesha
repository-code: "https://github.com/ujeshauchitha-glitch/RadSlice"
license: MIT
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-11-10"
keywords:
  - fault injection
  - fault tolerance
  - weight-memory protection
  - ECC
  - yield model
  - spare slices
  - RISC-V
  - FPGA
abstract: >-
  Preregistered simulation studies of how well weight-memory protections (ECC,
  scrubbing, checksum with reload) and array-level protections (duplication,
  triple voting, checksum lane) keep a small int8 classifier accurate under
  bit upsets, with a Verilog MAC array checked bit for bit against an integer
  reference; and an earlier study of spare-slice redundancy, boot self-test and
  remap for a slice-serial RV64I core under a 2D-semiconductor-style defect
  model, calibrated only to published register yields. All upset and defect
  rates are swept, tiered placeholders. No novelty is claimed and nothing is a
  measurement of a chip.
